timestamp=2026-11-04T09:16:37.297Z level=INFO run=5322f89d message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# We need exactly 10 company IDs. The top 10 by score:\n# 1. 2029 (9) - external giant, small footprint\n# 2. 6453 (9) - external giant, small footprint\n# 3. 8770 (8) - internal only-buyer, v weak\n# 4. 8285 (8) - internal only-buyer, v weak\n# 5. 1060 (8) - internal only-buyer, v weak\n# 6. 3495 (8) - internal only-buyer, v weak\n# 7. 7021 (8) - internal only-buyer, v weak\n# 8. 8382 (8) - benford weird, massive emp vs tiny rev, tiny sales\n# 9. 8810 (7) - internal only-buyer, v weak\n# 10. 4612 (6) - benford weird, repetitive small amounts\n# 11. 4521 (6) - internal only-buyer also weak but score 6\n\n# That gives us: 2029,6453,8770,8285,1060,3495,7021,8382,8810,4612 - 10 companies. That covers all 3 patterns well.\n# Also includes most of the only-buyers with weakest fundamentals (all except maybe 4521 which has score 6 vs others 7-8). 4521's bank age is 4, employees 18, rev 198k - also very weak; but score is 6. The other only-buyers have even weaker signals combined with other aspects? Or just scoring.\n\n# But let’s be precise about what “weak operating fundamentals” means in context. The only-buyers as a group are key. All 7 internal only-buyers are suspicious. In many fraud setups, many shell buyers exist. But we must pick 10 most likely to require investigation.\n\n# Comparing the 7: 8770,8285,1060,3495,7021 all have bank_age <= 3, employees < 40, declared revenue small relative to purchases? Also 8810 has bank_age 1 (very new). 4521 has bank_age 4 (slightly older). That’s why 4521 scores lower. So dropping 4521 in favor of keeping higher-scoring entities is reasonable.\n\n# The top 10 as listed look solid and cover: (a) 8382, 4612; (b) 8770, 8285, 1060, 3495, 7021, 8810 (6 only-buyers); (c) 2029, 6453. That’s 2+6+2=10 exactly. Perfect.\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
